From 34b378f9a0f4dd00cf998fff8f734fa7e8f0a991 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 02:19:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms, nvidia-drm: no Dolby Vision through a passive DP++
 adaptor that does not say HDMI

NVKMS asks the resource manager for the passive DisplayPort dual-mode
adaptor (DP++) behind the TMDS output of a DisplayPort connector
(DpyGetPassiveDpDongleType(), NV0073_CTRL_CMD_DFP_GET_DISPLAYPORT_DONGLE_INFO),
but only for the TMDS clock that it limits the display to
(nvDpyProbeMaxPixelClock()). Whether it sends HDMI there it decides by the
EDID (nvDpyIsHdmiEvo()), so the vendor InfoFrame of a client, the Dolby one
included, went out through an adaptor that RM reports as a DVI one: one
that does not answer at the I2C address of the dual-mode adaptors, of which
ctrl0073dfp.h says "clients MUST avoid outputting HDMI even if capable".
i915 and amdgpu refuse their Dolby Vision tunnel through such an adaptor
(linux-9995, linux-9998).

NVKMS now keeps the adaptor on the dpy with its maximum pixel clock, and
says each change of it on the TMDS output of a DisplayPort connector in the
kernel log, with the clock that it limits the display to. A flip or a mode
set that sends a Dolby Vision vendor InfoFrame (Standard or Low-Latency, or
the one of the HDMI 1.4b signalling) through the TMDS output of a
DisplayPort connector behind such an adaptor, or behind one of a kind that
this does not know, is refused, and the kernel log says why once for each
GPU and head until none is refused (nvHadesDolbyRefusedByAdaptor() of
nvkms-hades.h). nvidia-drm gives a dual-mode DisplayPort connector
NV_DUAL_MODE_ADAPTOR, read-only, set on detection where NV_DUAL_MODE_TMDS
is: while the display is driven through the TMDS output, 0 no adaptor
reported, 1 or 2 an HDMI adaptor of type 1 or 2, 3 one that does not
identify itself as an HDMI one, 255 where NVKMS did not say; 0 otherwise.
Kodi does not try native output where it says 3 (or a value it does not
know), and says why; the refusal of NVKMS stays as a guard.

Unchanged: an HDMI connector; the DisplayPort output of a connector; a
vendor InfoFrame that is not one of Dolby Vision (3D, HDR10+); an HDMI
adaptor, whose clock NVKMS already keeps; and the TMDS output of a
DisplayPort connector where RM reports no adaptor, as for the HDMI port of
a laptop wired to such an output through a level shifter (the HP ZBook 17
G6 of issue #4, whose kernel log has Dolby Vision at 3840x2160 and 594 MHz
there: RM keeps a DVI adaptor to 165 MHz, so on that port it reports none,
or an HDMI one). HDR10, HLG and sound through any adaptor go as NVKMS sends
them.

With the code before this, a DVI adaptor kept the display to 165 MHz
(TMDS_SINGLE_LINK_PCLK_MAX in nvDpyProbeMaxPixelClock()), and Hades sends
native Dolby Vision at 3840x2160 and 23.976 to 60 Hz only, which needs more
(297 MHz with the CTA timings): such a display had no mode for it but one
that it would list in YCbCr 4:2:0 only, which the RGB tunnel cannot carry.
The refusal is now by what the adaptor is, whatever the mode.

Tested on the host with nvkms-hades.h and nvidia-drm-connector.h as they
are: 29 and 9 checks; the six mutants of the rule (among them the code
before this change, and a rule that refuses where no adaptor is reported)
and the three of the value each fail. The five modules build against Linux
7.3.5 with the image's compiler, with no compiler warning that the series
without this change does not have. Not tried with an adaptor on hardware.
